  "summary": "I finished the RBC-PHARM-07 review for Harold Vance. One dose changed: gabapentin was too high for his kidney function and is now 600 mg twice daily. Apixaban and metformin stay the same. Note: the pharmacist's flag used a creatinine that belongs to another patient.\n\n**Values I used:**\n- **Creatinine:** 1.21 mg/dL from 10/20. The 1.88 result from 10/21 is marked entered-in-error (mislabelled specimen, another patient's blood), so I left it out.\n- **Weight:** 64.9 kg (143 lb), measured 10/20. The 132 lb entry from 10/22 was his own estimate by phone, not a measured weight.\n- **Age:** 82.\n\n**Each medication:**\n- **Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily – no change.** The policy's apixaban rule covers atrial fibrillation only. He takes it long-term to prevent another clot after his August 2025 pulmonary embolism. Hematology said in February that this is not a kidney-adjusted dose and should continue indefinitely. Even if the rule applied, he meets only one of the three criteria (age).\n- **Metformin 1000 mg twice daily – no change.** His kidney filtration estimate (eGFR) is 59.8, and the policy only calls for a change below 45.\n- **Gabapentin – changed.** His creatinine clearance is 43 mL/min, which allows 400–1400 mg/day given twice daily. He was on 600 mg three times daily (1800 mg/day). I changed the existing order to 600 mg twice daily (1200 mg/day), new order mr-t0001, same dispense terms.\n\nThe pharmacist's suggestions (metformin 500 mg twice daily, gabapentin 300 mg once daily) were based on the 1.88 result, so I didn't follow them. I left his other medications alone because the policy doesn't cover them.\n\n**Your note:** a signed progress note (doc-t0002) records the values, each rule's result, the change and the cited sources.\n\n**Still open:**\n- No one has told Mr. Vance about the gabapentin change yet. He should be told, and watched for more nerve pain or for sleepiness.\n- The pharmacist may also need to know that the 1.88 result wasn't his.",
